<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Herod Archelaus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">23 BCE – c. 18 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Herod Archelaus was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a> who ruled Judea, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idumaea">Idumaea</a> as ethnarch from 4 BCE to 6 CE, after Augustus denied him the royal title. His harsh rule provoked complaints from both Judeans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaritans">Samaritans</a>, and Augustus deposed him and exiled him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. Judea then came under the direct rule of Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prefect">prefects</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herod-archelaus.jpg" alt="Herod Archelaus" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georges Reverdy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Archelaus_medal.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Herod Archelaus was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a> and Malthace the Samaritan, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-antipas">Herod Antipas</a> and half-brother of Herod II. His name means leading the people. Shortly before his death, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>, Herod the Great had been involved in a religious crisis: he had placed a golden eagle over the Temple entrance, which was seen as blasphemous, and it was chopped down with axes. Two teachers and about 40 youths were arrested for the act and burned. Archelaus came to power at his father's death in 4 BCE, and ruled over one half of his father's territories.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> describes Archelaus before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a> of 4 BCE, dressed in white and seated on a golden throne. He appeared kind to the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, who wanted lower taxes and an end to the imprisonment of Herod's enemies. The crowd then demanded punishment for those who had ordered the deaths of the two teachers and 40 youths, and the replacement of Herod's High Priest by one of greater piety and purity. Archelaus agreed to the latter and asked for moderation until Augustus confirmed him as king.</p><p>As the crowd in the Temple area grew threatening, he sent a general and a tribune to reason with them; they were stoned, and many were killed. After midnight Archelaus sent the whole army into the city, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> records a death toll of 3000. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a> was cancelled. The army proclaimed him king, but he declined the title until Augustus had heard his claims, and he sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. There his brother Antipas accused him over the uproar and disputed the succession, relying on an earlier will. Nicolaus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> argued for Archelaus, and Augustus divided the kingdom, giving him Judea with the title of ethnarch.</p><p>Archelaus divorced his first wife, Mariamne, to marry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glaphyra">Glaphyra</a>, the widow of his brother Alexander. Her second husband, Juba, king of Mauretania, was still alive, and his subjects complained to Augustus that the marriage broke the Mosaic law. Archelaus fell into disrepute and was deposed in the tenth year of his rule and banished to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. His lands became the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Archelaus appears in the Gospel of Matthew, in which Joseph, returning from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, hears that Archelaus rules <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a> and is afraid to go there, and turns aside to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a>; this is Matthew's explanation of why <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> grew up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazareth">Nazareth</a>. Some interpreters think the opening of the parable of the minas in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke">Gospel of Luke</a> refers to his journey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, while others see a later adaptation in oral tradition.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Herod Archelaus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Herod the Great's will named <b>Archelaus</b> as heir to the kingship, but Augustus in 4 BCE gave him only the title ethnarch and the heartland of the kingdom: Judea with Jerusalem, Samaria and Idumaea, about half of his father's territory, with the promise of a crown if he…</blockquote><a href="/state/herodian-ethnarchy-of-judea">Herodian Ethnarchy of Judea</a></li><li><blockquote>After Herod's death in 4 BCE, Augustus divided the kingdom among his sons <b>Archelaus</b>, Herod Antipas and Philip.</blockquote><a href="/state/herodian-kingdom-of-judea">Herodian Kingdom of Judea</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.5,2.9&amp;year=3" aria-label="Gaul on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4618 529 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Herod Archelaus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5068 799h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5068 799h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=46.5,2.9&amp;year=3">What was here in 3 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
